WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Here's a bottle of household ammonia cleaner.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.920
Touch it with red litmus paper, and the

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.000
paper turns blue.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.800
So it's a base.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.440
But look at the formula: N H three.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.120
One nitrogen, three hydrogens, and no oxygen at

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.520
all, so there's no hydroxide in it.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.560
For decades, the leading theory said a base

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.960
had to supply its own hydroxide.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.520
So how can ammonia be a base?

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:30.173
To answer that, follow how the idea changed.

00:00:30.494 --> 00:00:33.293
In chemistry, a model is a definition that

00:00:33.454 --> 00:00:36.093
predicts which substances will act as acids or

00:00:36.174 --> 00:00:37.053
bases.

00:00:37.374 --> 00:00:39.613
A model gets replaced when someone finds an

00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:42.093
observation it can't explain.

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:44.253
That's happened three times in about a hundred

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:47.293
and fifty years, and each new model fixed

00:00:47.374 --> 00:00:48.973
the failure of the one before.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.720
In the late seventeen hundreds, Antoine Lavoisier studied

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.440
sulfuric, nitric and phosphoric acid.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.160
Every one contained oxygen, so he decided oxygen

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.440
is what makes an acid.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.560
He even named the element for it: oxygen

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.000
means "acid maker".

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.480
Then, in 1810, Humphry Davy showed that hydrochloric

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.360
acid contains only hydrogen and chlorine.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.560
No oxygen.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.680
One acid without oxygen was enough to break

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.320
the rule.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:23.973
So Davy proposed that hydrogen, not oxygen, is

00:01:24.054 --> 00:01:25.813
the key ingredient in acids.

00:01:26.374 --> 00:01:29.173
But hydrogen alone doesn't make something an acid.

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:31.973
Methane is full of hydrogen, and it isn't

00:01:32.134 --> 00:01:32.533
acidic.

00:01:33.094 --> 00:01:35.493
And the idea said nothing about bases.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.820
In the eighteen eighties, Svante Arrhenius brought in

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.940
ions.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.420
He said an acid is a substance that

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.780
breaks apart in water to release hydrogen ions,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.780
and a base breaks apart in water to

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.580
release its own hydroxide ions.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.900
Hydrogen chloride gives hydrogen ions, and sodium hydroxide

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.980
gives hydroxide ions.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.260
Mix them, and hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.020
join to make water.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.780
That explained neutralisation, and why so many acids

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.060
behave alike: they all release the same ion.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:13.826
And the Arrhenius model still works for acids

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.666
and hydroxide bases in water.

00:02:16.227 --> 00:02:17.586
But back to ammonia.

00:02:18.147 --> 00:02:21.506
Under Arrhenius, a base releases its own hydroxide

00:02:21.587 --> 00:02:24.106
ions, the way sodium hydroxide does.

00:02:24.387 --> 00:02:26.386
Ammonia has none to release.

00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:29.666
To make it fit, chemists kept writing ammonia

00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:33.746
solution as "ammonium hydroxide", N H four O

00:02:33.907 --> 00:02:34.306
H.

00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:37.346
But no one has ever isolated a substance

00:02:37.427 --> 00:02:40.306
made of ammonium ions and hydroxide ions.

00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:43.266
The model needed a made-up compound to cover

00:02:43.347 --> 00:02:43.906
a gap.

00:02:45.134 --> 00:02:46.813
Two more problems piled up.

00:02:47.294 --> 00:02:50.653
Baking soda and washing soda make water basic,

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:53.133
and they don't contain hydroxide either.

00:02:53.454 --> 00:02:56.173
And when hydrogen chloride gas and ammonia gas

00:02:56.254 --> 00:02:58.893
meet inside a glass tube, a white ring

00:02:58.974 --> 00:03:01.573
of solid ammonium chloride forms.

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:03.773
That's an acid and a base making a

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:04.333
salt.

00:03:04.894 --> 00:03:07.653
The gases react directly, without water as the

00:03:07.694 --> 00:03:10.893
reaction medium, so a definition limited to reactions

00:03:10.974 --> 00:03:13.453
in aqueous solution can't describe it.

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:19.146
In 1923, two chemists, Johannes Brønsted in Copenhagen

00:03:19.387 --> 00:03:22.906
and Thomas Lowry in Cambridge, independently published the

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:24.186
same fix.

00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:27.066
Stop asking which ions appear in water.

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:29.106
Ask what moves.

00:03:29.387 --> 00:03:32.186
An acid is a proton donor: it gives

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:33.866
away a hydrogen ion.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.066
A base is a proton acceptor: it takes

00:03:37.187 --> 00:03:37.386
one.

00:03:38.027 --> 00:03:40.346
A hydrogen ion is a hydrogen atom that

00:03:40.427 --> 00:03:43.226
has lost its only electron, so it's just

00:03:43.254 --> 00:03:44.026
a proton.

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:46.433
Watch it work.

00:03:46.674 --> 00:03:49.153
Hydrogen chloride dissolves in water.

00:03:49.474 --> 00:03:52.513
One hydrogen moves, as a proton, onto a

00:03:52.594 --> 00:03:53.673
water molecule.

00:03:53.954 --> 00:03:57.313
So hydrogen chloride is the acid, the donor,

00:03:57.394 --> 00:04:00.073
and water is the base, the acceptor.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.833
Water with an extra proton is H three

00:04:02.994 --> 00:04:05.393
O plus, the hydronium ion.

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:09.393
That's what H plus, aqueous, really means: a

00:04:09.474 --> 00:04:12.433
proton in water is always attached to water.

00:04:13.634 --> 00:04:14.673
Now the hook.

00:04:14.914 --> 00:04:18.273
Ammonia dissolves in water, and this time water

00:04:18.354 --> 00:04:19.273
is the donor.

00:04:19.554 --> 00:04:22.353
A water molecule gives a proton to ammonia,

00:04:22.434 --> 00:04:24.273
which becomes an ammonium ion.

00:04:24.754 --> 00:04:26.593
What's left of the water molecule is a

00:04:26.674 --> 00:04:27.953
hydroxide ion.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.633
The hydroxide comes from water, not from ammonia.

00:04:32.274 --> 00:04:34.673
Only some ammonia molecules do this at any

00:04:34.754 --> 00:04:37.073
moment, which is why the equation has the

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:39.273
double arrow you met in equilibrium.

00:04:39.554 --> 00:04:42.113
Ammonia is a base because it accepts a

00:04:42.234 --> 00:04:42.833
proton.

00:04:44.034 --> 00:04:46.433
The other two problems fall the same way.

00:04:46.674 --> 00:04:49.313
In the glass tube, hydrogen chloride hands a

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.353
proton directly to ammonia.

00:04:51.554 --> 00:04:52.753
No water needed.

00:04:52.994 --> 00:04:55.793
The ammonium ions and chloride ions that form

00:04:56.114 --> 00:04:58.153
pack together as the white solid.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:00.673
And a carbonate ion in water takes a

00:05:00.754 --> 00:05:04.033
proton from a water molecule, leaving hydroxide behind.

00:05:04.594 --> 00:05:06.913
So carbonates are bases for the same reason

00:05:06.994 --> 00:05:09.953
ammonia is: they're proton acceptors.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:12.846
Notice what water did.

00:05:13.087 --> 00:05:16.766
With hydrogen chloride, water accepted a proton, so

00:05:16.847 --> 00:05:17.966
it was the base.

00:05:18.207 --> 00:05:21.246
With ammonia, water gave a proton, so it

00:05:21.327 --> 00:05:22.246
was the acid.

00:05:22.527 --> 00:05:25.086
In this model, acid and base are roles

00:05:25.127 --> 00:05:27.806
in a particular reaction, not fixed labels on

00:05:27.887 --> 00:05:28.366
a bottle.

00:05:28.847 --> 00:05:31.086
To decide, follow the proton.

00:05:31.327 --> 00:05:33.526
The species that loses a proton is the

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:34.046
acid.

00:05:34.447 --> 00:05:36.846
The species that gains it is the base.

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:40.026
Even this model has a limit.

00:05:40.307 --> 00:05:43.746
Calcium oxide and sulfur trioxide react to make

00:05:43.827 --> 00:05:46.066
calcium sulfate, a salt.

00:05:46.387 --> 00:05:49.186
In water, these oxides make a base and

00:05:49.227 --> 00:05:49.906
an acid.

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:52.706
But there isn't a single hydrogen atom in

00:05:52.787 --> 00:05:55.666
either reactant, so no proton can move.

00:05:56.227 --> 00:05:59.266
The Brønsted-Lowry model can't call this an acid-base

00:05:59.347 --> 00:06:00.266
reaction.

00:06:00.627 --> 00:06:03.506
A later idea, the Lewis model, covers cases

00:06:03.587 --> 00:06:06.146
like this, but it's beyond this course.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.166
So here's how to remember it.

00:06:09.447 --> 00:06:12.326
First: each model fixed a failure of the

00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:13.206
one before.

00:06:13.687 --> 00:06:16.326
Oxygen, broken by hydrochloric acid.

00:06:16.807 --> 00:06:18.966
Hydrogen, not enough on its own.

00:06:19.447 --> 00:06:22.086
Ions in water, limited by ammonia.

00:06:22.727 --> 00:06:27.366
Second, for Brønsted-Lowry: acid gives, base takes.

00:06:27.607 --> 00:06:29.446
And when a question asks you to assess

00:06:29.487 --> 00:06:32.086
a model, say what it explained, then the

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:34.006
example it couldn't explain.

00:06:35.234 --> 00:06:35.873
Your turn.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:39.713
The equation shows the hydrogen carbonate ion reacting

00:06:39.794 --> 00:06:40.673
with water.

00:06:40.994 --> 00:06:45.233
Identify the Brønsted-Lowry acid and the Brønsted-Lowry base

00:06:45.314 --> 00:06:46.833
in the forward reaction.

00:06:47.154 --> 00:06:49.953
Justify your answer in terms of proton transfer.

00:06:50.514 --> 00:06:51.793
Pause here to answer.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.680
Follow the proton.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.560
Water becomes hydroxide, so it lost a proton.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.320
Water is the acid.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.440
The hydrogen carbonate ion becomes H two C

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.880
O three, so it gained a proton.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.200
It's the base.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.920
If you picked hydrogen carbonate as the acid

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.560
because its formula has a hydrogen, that's the

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:22.800
classic slip.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.840
Having a hydrogen doesn't make something an acid.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:27.720
Giving one away does.

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:29.573
Last one.

00:07:29.654 --> 00:07:32.853
A student states: "The Arrhenius model has been

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:35.173
proved wrong, so it should no longer be

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:35.813
used."

00:07:36.374 --> 00:07:38.373
Assess the validity of this statement.

00:07:38.854 --> 00:07:40.773
Refer to ammonia in your answer.

00:07:41.414 --> 00:07:42.853
Pause here to answer.

00:07:53.267 --> 00:07:55.146
The statement is not valid.

00:07:55.347 --> 00:07:56.906
Arrhenius isn't wrong.

00:07:57.107 --> 00:08:00.066
It correctly describes acids and hydroxide bases in

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:02.506
water, and it's still useful there.

00:08:02.787 --> 00:08:05.426
Its limitation is that it needs water, and

00:08:05.507 --> 00:08:07.986
a base with its own hydroxide, so it

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:10.626
can't explain why ammonia is a base.

00:08:10.867 --> 00:08:15.186
Brønsted-Lowry can: ammonia accepts a proton from water.

00:08:15.667 --> 00:08:17.506
"Proved wrong" is the mistake.

00:08:17.907 --> 00:08:19.186
Write "limited".

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.040
So whenever you meet a reaction, follow the

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.160
proton: the species that loses one is the

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.240
acid, and the one that gains it is

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.800
the base, in water or not.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.960
And to judge any model, say what it

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.520
explained, then what broke it.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.280
Next: naming acids and bases, and predicting what

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.800
they make when they react.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.440
Here are the key notes.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:45.600
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:46.480
to copy them.
